<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Hoverboards and Magic Carpets</title>

  <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                <term>The setting:</term> You are a young traveler, leaving home
                for the first time.
                Your parents want to help you on your journey, so just before
                your departure, they give you two gifts.
                Specifically, they give you two forms of transportation: a
                hoverboard and a magic carpet.
                Your parents inform you that the hoverboard and the magic carpet
                have restrictions in how they operate.
            </p>

            <p>
                <term>Hoverboard's Restrictions:</term> We denote the
                restriction on the hoverboard's movement by the vector
                <m>\vvec = \twovec{3}{1}</m>.
                By this we mean that if the hoverboard traveled "forward" for
                one hour, it would move along a "diagonal" path that would
                result in a displacement of 3 miles East and 1 mile North of its
                starting location.
                You can also put it in reverse - after one hour you'd be 3 miles
                West and 1 mile South.
            </p>

            <p>
                <term>Magic Carpet's Restrictions:</term> We denote the
                restriction on the magic carpet's movement by the vector
                <m>\wvec = \twovec{1}{2}</m>.
                By this we mean that if the magic carpet traveled "forward" for
                one hour, it would move along a "diagonal" path that would
                result in a displacement of 1 mile East and 2 miles North of its
                starting location.
                You can also take this in reverse as well.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace=".2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the tape to mark the origin and <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>
                    axis.
                    Each tile is one unit.
                    You've made a "floor plane."
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="08-solutions">
                <image>
                    <shortdescription>Floor plane.</shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="09-2-3_1">
                        <diagram dimensions="(300,250)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-5,-5,5,5)">
                                <grid-axes vticks="(-5,1,5)" hticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-4,2,4)" vlabels="(-4,2,4)" spacings="((-10,1,10),(-10,1,10))"/>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Travel with the hoverboard for one hour, and then travel
                    with the magic carpet forward for one hour.
                    Where do you end up?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="08-solutions">
                <image>
                    <shortdescription>
                        One hour of travel with the hoverboard and then one hour
                        of travel with the magic carpet.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="09-2-3_2">
                        <diagram dimensions="(300,250)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-5,-5,5,5)">
                                <grid-axes vticks="(-5,1,5)" hticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-4,2,4)" vlabels="(-4,2,4)" spacings="((-10,1,10),(-10,1,10))"/>
                                <vector v="(3,1)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="blue" alignment="nw">
                                    <m>
                                        {\mathbf v}
                                    </m>
                                </vector>
                                <vector v="(1,2)" tail="(3,1)" stroke="orange" alignment="se">
                                    <m>
                                        {\mathbf w}
                                    </m>
                                </vector>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>

                <p>
                    <m>\twovec{3}{1} + \twovec{1}{2} = \twovec{4}{3}</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Now do the opposite - travel with the magic carpet forward
                    for one hour, and then travel with the hoverboard forward
                    for one hour.
                    Where do you end up?
                    What does this say about <m>\vvec+\wvec</m> vs.
                    <m>\wvec+\vvec</m>?
                    Does this hold in general?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="08-solutions">
                <image>
                    <shortdescription>
                        One hour of travel with the magic carpet and then one
                        hour of travel with the hoverboard.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="09-2-3_3">
                        <diagram dimensions="(300,250)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-5,-5,5,5)">
                                <grid-axes vticks="(-5,1,5)" hticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-4,2,4)" vlabels="(-4,2,4)" spacings="((-10,1,10),(-10,1,10))"/>
                                <vector v="(3,1)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="blue" alignment="nw">
                                    <m>
                                        {\mathbf v}
                                    </m>
                                </vector>
                                <vector v="(1,2)" tail="(3,1)" stroke="orange" alignment="se">
                                    <m>
                                        {\mathbf w}
                                    </m>
                                </vector>
                                <vector v="(1,2)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="orange" alignment="nw">
                                    <m>
                                        {\mathbf w}
                                    </m>
                                </vector>
                                <vector v="(3,1)" tail="(1,2)" stroke="blue" alignment="nw">
                                    <m>
                                        {\mathbf v}
                                    </m>
                                </vector>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>

                <p>
                    <m>\twovec{1}{2} + \twovec{3}{1} = \twovec{4}{3}</m>.
                    So, <m>\vvec + \wvec = \wvec + \vvec</m> and this holds in
                    general!
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Where do you end up if you travel in the reverse direction
                    on your magic carpet for 1.5 hours?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="08-solutions">
                <image>
                    <shortdescription>
                        Traveling with the magic carpet for 1.5 hours in the
                        reverse direction.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="09-2-3_4">
                        <diagram dimensions="(300,250)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-5,-5,5,5)">
                                <grid-axes vticks="(-5,1,5)" hticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-4,2,4)" vlabels="(-4,2,4)" spacings="((-10,1,10),(-10,1,10))"/>
                                <vector v="(-1.5)*(1,2)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="orange" alignment="nw">
                                    <m>
                                        {\mathbf w}
                                    </m>
                                </vector>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>

                <p>
                    <m>(-1.5)\twovec{1}{2} = \twovec{-1.5}{-3}</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    You lost your magic carpet.
                    Starting at the origin and using just your hoverboard, can
                    you get to your friend's house at <m>\twovec{-6}{-2}</m>?
                    Can you get to the grocery store at <m>\twovec{2}{4}</m>?
                    How would you describe the collection of places you can get
                    to?
                    (I.e., is it a point or a line or all of
                    <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>?)
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="08-solutions">
                <p>
                    To get to your friend's house, you need to travel backwards
                    for 2 hours.
                    <m>(-2)\twovec{3}{1} = \twovec{-6}{-2}</m>.
                    But, you can't get to the grocery store because there is no
                    value <m>x</m> such that
                    <m>x\twovec{3}{1} = \twovec{3x}{x} = \twovec{2}{4}</m>.
                    Therefore, <m>\twovec{3x}{x} \neq \twovec{2}{4}</m> for any
                    <m>x</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Good news, you're back at the origin, and you have both your
                    magic carpet and your hoverboard.
                    You want to go visit the wise man, Old Man Gauss, who lives
                    in a cabin that is 107 miles East and 64 miles North of your
                    home.
                    Is it possible to get there?
                    If so, express how using a linear combination/vector
                    notation.
                    If not, explain why not.
                    Is there more than one way to get there?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="08-solutions">
                <p>
                    To answer this, let's set up a linear combination and solve
                    the corresponding linear system!
                    First,
                    <md>
                        x_1\twovec{3}{1} + x_2\twovec{1}{2} = \twovec{107}{64}.
                    </md>
                    Then,
                    <md>
                        \left[
    \begin{array}{rr|r}
      3 \amp  1 \amp 107 \\
      1 \amp  2 \amp 64 \\
    \end{array}
  \right] \sim \left[
    \begin{array}{rr|r}
      1 \amp  0 \amp 30 \\
      0 \amp  1 \amp 17 \\
    \end{array}
  \right]
                    </md>
                    So, 30 hours on the hoverboard and 17 on the magic carpet
                    will get you there!
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Old Man Gauss now tries to hide somewhere you can't visit
                    him.
                    Is there somewhere he could hide?
                    How would you describe a collection of places you can get
                    to?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="08-solutions">
                <p>
                    He can't hide.
                    Consider the augmented system
                    <md>
                        \left[
    \begin{array}{rr|r}
      3 \amp  1 \amp x \\
      1 \amp  2 \amp y \\
    \end{array}
  \right] \sim \left[
    \begin{array}{rr|r}
      1 \amp  0 \amp * \\
      0 \amp  1 \amp * \\
    \end{array}
  \right]
                    </md>
                    This system is always consistent and therefore, always has a
                    solutions!
                    We can get anywhere in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Your cousin also gets a magic carpet and a hoverboard from
                    your aunt and uncle.
                    His magic carpet also travels in the direction
                    <m>\vvec = \twovec{3}{1}</m>, but his hoverboard travels in
                    the direction <m>\uvec = \twovec{6}{2}</m>.
                    Use your "floor plane" to determine where your cousin can
                    go.
                    Can Old Man Gauss hide from your friend?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="08-solutions">
                <p>
                    My cousin can only travel along a line since <m>\vvec</m>
                    and <m>\uvec</m> are scalar multiples of each other.
                    Specifically, my cousin only travels along the line
                    <m>{y = \frac{1}{3}x}</m>.
                </p>

                <image>
                    <shortdescription>
                        Graph of
                        a function going
                        through the 3 given points.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="09-2-3_5">
                        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
                            <definition>
                                f(x)=(1/3)*x
                            </definition>
                            <coordinates bbox="(-2,-2,8,4)">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
                                <graph function="f"/>
                                <point p="(3,1)" alignment="nw">
                                    <m>
                                        (3,1)
                                    </m>
                                </point>
                                <point p="(6,2)" alignment="nw">
                                    <m>
                                        (6,2)
                                    </m>
                                </point>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>